ID: B3-Ch06c-Explanation
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch6_average,1) if (v_agility == 0 && v_ancient_languages == 0 && v_aura_hardening == 0 && v_bluff == 0 && v_combat_technique == 0 && v_hearing == 0 && v_magical_sense == 0 && v_perception == 0 && v_premonition == 0 && v_reflexes == 0 && v_strength == 0 && v_toughness == 0)
achievement("Average is my middle name",v_ac_b3_ch6_average)
#if(v_b3_ch6_kelrim != 1 && v_b3_ch6_plan != 3) {
 "We should get back to the waiting room as well," Daren says. "See you later, Barry. Thanks for the pull."

"Any time," the teleporter operator says, and we file out of the chamber, heading down the tunnel toward the rebel base.

As we walk, white light flares along Daren's palms as he tends to everyone who caught a blow during the fight.

"So, uh..." Hadrik speaks up, watching Daren weave a soothing glow over Leila's bruised shoulder. "I suppose I owe you all an explanation about what Gontrok kept shouting at me back there."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_plan == 3 && v_b3_ch6_kelrim != 1) {
 "Everyone, I have chores waiting at the house, so I'm closing the channel now," Rose's voice comes through the transceiver. "Try to stay out of trouble for the rest of the day, all right? I won't be able to hold time again until I get some sleep."

"We'll keep our heads down," I tell her. "Rest up, and thanks again."

The line clicks dead. White light flares along Daren's palms as he tends to everyone who caught a blow during the fight, keeping pace with us as we march toward the waiting room.

"So, uh..." Hadrik speaks up, watching Daren weave a soothing glow over Leila's bruised shoulder. "I suppose I owe you all an explanation about what Gontrok kept shouting at me back there."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_kelrim == 1) {
 "Kate, I've been meaning to ask," Melindra says as we enter the stone corridor. "How did you manage to unravel the captain's aura technique earlier? Have you been doing extra drills behind my back?"

"Not behind your back," Kate says. "You watched me glue an icicle to my palm with my aura this morning."

"Right, you did that," Melindra says. "I almost forgot. But was that really enough to figure out how to counter the captain?"

"No," Kate says. "Daren mentioned yesterday that untangling an aura is like untying knots. Took me a while to translate what he meant, but luckily I worked it out before the captain crushed the life out of us."

"You pieced that together just from that?" Daren asks, genuinely impressed.

"Barely," Kate says. "Next time you try explaining abstract theory, pick a better metaphor."

"I'll work on it," Daren says.

White light flares along his palms as he tends to everyone who caught a blow during the fight, keeping pace with us as we march toward the waiting room.

"So, uh..." Hadrik speaks up, watching Daren weave a soothing glow over Leila's bruised shoulder. "I suppose I owe you all an explanation about what Gontrok kept shouting at me back there."

}
 "Only if you want to tell it," Daren tells him.

"Yeah." Hadrik looks aside into the dark stone for a second. "Yeah, I think I'm ready."

"Oh, you're finally going to tell them about the dwarf village and the boulders?" Arraka's voice cuts in from the golden amulet at Flower's chest. "About damn time. Keeping your little secret was becoming more trouble than it was worth."

"You knew?" Hadrik stares down at the amulet, dumbfounded.

"Of course I knew," Arraka says. "Did you honestly think I would skip your trial, of all people? Yours was the first one I dropped in on. That makes two ales you owe me now, dwarf. One for pulling your neck out of the ogre captain's grip, and one for keeping my mouth shut about Gontrok and the dwarven village. Next time I'm out of this amulet, you're paying up."

"Aye," Hadrik says, a grim half-grin tugging at his beard. "I'll make sure a few bottles of dwarven ale are waiting."

He falls silent for a second, then clears his throat, turning back to the rest of us. "So, where do I start? Do you remember when I mentioned I was the black sheep of my clan?"

"I remember!" Flower says. "You said the reason your family hated you had nothing to do with your height!"

"Right," Hadrik says. "Well, the reason is simple enough, and it's tied directly to what the God of Time dragged me through during the ritual. To start from the beginning... Gontrok and a few other clanmates shook me awake in the middle of the night. They told me about a dwarven settlement that was supposedly serving as a staging post for a strike team preparing to hit our camp in a couple of days. This was fifty years back, in the thick of a bloody war with the dwarves, so I had no reason to think they were lying to me."

"So it wasn't a garrison at all?" Daren asks. "Just a civilian settlement?"

"Aye," Hadrik says. "Just a quiet dwarven village without a single warrior in it. I should have realized something was wrong when I saw no sentries posted on the perimeter, but I convinced myself they were just hiding in the brush, waiting to ambush us. Gontrok insisted we needed to strike before they had the chance to move. He led us to the crest of a cliff overlooking the valley where the village sat. At the top were massive boulders—stones so heavy nobody but giants could shift them. Gontrok wanted us to heave them all over the edge at once, so they'd roll down into the valley and flatten the houses before anyone inside could wake up."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06c-Explanation2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06c-Explanation2)


ID: B3-Ch06c-Explanation2
TEXT:

 "So that was your ritual," Kate says quietly.

"Aye," Hadrik says, his voice thick and uncharacteristically subdued. "To pass the trial, I had to heave that boulder off the ridge just like the first time. Had to crush all those innocent folk again. I didn't know that going in. Like the rest of you, I thought the god was giving me a second chance to fix my worst mistake. Took me hours of bloody failure to see what the ritual actually demanded. And it wasn't just rolling the stone. I had to walk down into the rubble afterward, check every flattened home for survivors that weren't there, and look directly at every body. Every crushed face. Women, children. Those faces haunted my dreams for years, and I'd fought hard to forget them. The ritual forced me to count every one."

"Gods..." Flower's voice trembles. "That's awful. But how did that turn your own family against you?"

"When I marched back into camp that night, I was beside myself," Hadrik says. "First thing I did was storm into the chieftain's pavilion to turn myself in, along with Gontrok and the others, for murder. The chief laughed in my teeth. Told me our clan's rules of war only applied to battles with other giants, not to vermin like the dwarves. Told me those children would have grown into enemy fighters anyway, so crushing them in their sleep was doing the clan a favor. That was the day I learned what our people's grand talk about honor was actually worth. I couldn't swallow it. I demanded tribunals, demanded justice for the dead, until the clan stopped laughing and turned hostile. Called me a traitor and a dwarf-sympathizer. Said I'd thrown away my right to call myself a warrior."

"Is that why Gontrok said your parents think you have no honor?" Daren asks.

"Part of it," Hadrik says. "The rest came when I realized no justice would ever come from my clan. I packed my kit and walked away into self-imposed exile. I shrank down into the shape of a dwarf, crossed into the northern kingdoms, and went hunting for the families of the folk I'd crushed. Walking out on the clan like that guaranteed I'd be executed on sight if I ever showed my face in the giant lands again. Didn't care. All I wanted was to balance the ledger."

"Did you find any of them?" Kate asks.

"Aye," Hadrik says. "A few of the folk in that valley had only been passing through, leaving their children behind in neighboring towns. When I tracked them down, I took those children in. Raised them under my own roof until they were grown, along with other orphans the war had left in the dirt. Decades rolled on. The war ground to a halt. Those kids grew up, found trades, took wives and husbands, built families of their own. But with no home waiting for me in the giant territories, I stayed in the north. Kept the dwarf skin on."

"Did none of those dwarves ever realize what you were?" Melindra asks.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06c-Explanation3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06c-Explanation3)


ID: B3-Ch06c-Explanation3
TEXT:

 "Most caught on eventually," Hadrik says, "but by then I'd lived among them for so long they didn't care. After decades in the mountains, I talked like them, drank like them, thought like them. To most of the town, there wasn't any difference worth mentioning. The only thing that chafed was the fighting. Giant blood doesn't sit quiet, and pulling my punches in bar brawls so I wouldn't accidentally cave someone's ribs in got suffocating. That's what drove me out on the road—wandering the world looking for monsters and mages who could take a full-strength punch without coming apart. I always drifted back to the northern taverns, but even after all these years, I've never had the stomach to tell those orphans why I put on the dwarf skin in the first place. Maybe one day. Maybe when I find the words."

Hadrik's voice trails off into the damp chill of the passage. Our boots have stopped scraping the floor. We stand in a quiet circle along the stone tunnel, nobody making a move to walk on.

Daren steps forward, his heavy plate clinking softly, and rests a broad hand on Hadrik's shoulder. "Hadrik. Pour us two glasses of dwarven ale. Small cups, like the ones from Flower's birthday. I need a clear head for the healing wards, but..."

Hadrik blinks up at him, his mouth falling open under his dark beard. "Did the righteous healer just ask for a drink before sundown? You never touch ale before dark!"

"I can make an exception," Daren says gently. "You look like you could use one."

The surprise softens on Hadrik's face, replaced by a genuine, weary grin. He unbuckles his leather pack and slides a heavy clay bottle from the padded lining.

"You aren't wrong about that," Hadrik says. "Here, hold the neck while I dig out the cups. Unless you want one too, Barry? Joining in?"

choice("Drink a glass of dwarven ale with Daren and Hadrik.", B3-Ch06c-Drink, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06c-Drink, v_b3_ch6_drink = 1)
choice("Don't drink with them.", B3-Ch06c-Drink, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06c-Drink, v_b3_ch6_drink = 0)


ID: B3-Ch06c-Drink
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch6_drink == 1) {
 "Sure, I'll take a glass," I say.

"Good man," Hadrik says. He passes small clay cups to Daren and me, uncorks the jug, and pours a dark, foaming stream to the brim of each. "In that case, a toast to running the royal guard out of our hair. Would have been sweeter if I'd broken Gontrok's neck, but making him flee under a teleport ward isn't nothing. To victory!"

"To victory," Daren and I say together.

We knock back the thick, bitter ale in a single swallow.

"Right, time to move," Hadrik says, stowing the cups and jug back into his pack. "We've kept those folk in the quarantine ward waiting long enough."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_drink == 0) {
 "I'll pass for now," I say. "Need my wits about me in case another patrol ambushes us."

"Suit yourself," Hadrik says. He hands Daren a small clay cup, uncorks the jug, and fills it with dark, foaming ale before pouring his own. "In that case, Daren, this toast is for you finally breaking your daylight-sobriety vow and joining me for a drink. Maybe there's hope for you yet, and one day you'll appreciate the dwarven art of drinking whenever the mood strikes, eh? What do you say? Hah!"

"Don't hold your breath," Daren says with a dry grin.

Their cups clink together, and both of them drain the thick ale in a single gulp.

"Right, time to move," Hadrik says, stowing the cups and jug back into his pack. "We've kept those folk in the quarantine ward waiting long enough."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_kelrim != 1 && v_b3_ch6_plan != 3) {
 We follow the winding stone corridor until it opens into the main waiting room. Daren splits off immediately, heading through the reinforced doorway into the quarantine ward. While we wait for him to finish treating the sick, Kate slips down a deserted side passage to resume drills with Melindra, while Hadrik and Flower find quiet alcoves in the base for their own practice.

"I'm going to check on my father in his workshop," Leila's blue script forms in the air before me. "Want to come?"

"Sure," I say. "Lead on."

We turn into the corridor leading toward Doctor Reidzek's workshop, keeping a relaxed pace. As we walk, Leila's bright blue letters glide along in midair just ahead of my chest, moving in rhythm with our steps so I can read them clearly.

"Walking through underground passages with you brings back memories," she writes with a small smile. "Feels like being back underneath our division cells in Thilias, looking for ways to rig the games."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_kelrim == 1 || v_b3_ch6_plan == 3) {
 We follow the winding stone corridor until it opens into the main waiting room. Daren splits off immediately, heading through the reinforced doorway into the quarantine ward alongside Kelrim. While we wait for them to finish treating the sick, Kate slips down a deserted side passage to resume drills with Melindra, while Hadrik and Flower find quiet alcoves in the base for their own practice.

"I'm going to check on my father in his workshop," Leila's blue script forms in the air before me. "Want to come?"

"Sure," I say. "Lead on."

We turn into the corridor leading toward Doctor Reidzek's workshop, keeping a relaxed pace. As we walk, Leila's bright blue letters glide along in midair just ahead of my chest, moving in rhythm with our steps so I can read them clearly.

"Walking through underground passages with you brings back memories," she writes with a small smile. "Feels like being back underneath our division cells in Thilias, looking for ways to rig the games."

}
 "A little nostalgic, yeah," I say. "Except for the part where the arena master was listening through the walls the entire time. Could have lived without that revelation."

"And almost drowning in a subterranean cistern," Leila adds in glowing script. "Not an experience I plan on repeating."

"Right, that," I say. "You really ought to look into swimming lessons. Varathian cities have public baths and pools, don't they?"

"They do," Leila writes. "A few in Ollendor, too. Maybe you can teach me sometime. You looked like you actually knew what you were doing in the water."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06c-Swimming, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06c-Swimming)


ID: B3-Ch06c-Swimming
TEXT:

 "I was useless in the water myself until I went spelunking with an archaeology professor on the western continent and a cave-in sealed us in," I say. "The only way out was a submerged tunnel three hundred paces long. Neither of us could swim worth a damn, so we spent hours on a narrow rock shelf practicing breath-holding and kicking techniques until we could cover distance without drowning. Funniest part is that was the simplest obstacle in that cavern."

"Another one of your 'ordinary' jobs from your drifting days?" Leila writes, a smirk showing at the corner of her mouth.

"All right, fair point," I say. "Maybe some of those odd jobs were less dull than I let on. Do you want to hear the rest or not?"

"Go ahead," she writes with a grin, matching her stride to mine as we approach her father's workshop.

By the time the tale wraps up, we reach Doctor Reidzek's heavy wooden door. I rap on the timber several times, but no sound comes from within. I call his name through the latch; silence answers. With the workshop clearly empty, we settle down outside to wait.

We sit side by side on the flagstones with our backs against the rough wood, trading stories from before the tournament. Leila describes life in the forest cabin after Reidzek smuggled her out of the Beacon, and I tell her about the libraries, odd jobs, and eccentric scholars I ran across while drifting through the Western Continent.

Talking with Leila carries an effortless rhythm. There's no posturing, no guards to keep up, just an easy exchange that makes the minutes slip by unnoticed.

"It feels like forever since we could just sit and talk without a disaster breathing down our necks," Leila writes, stretching her arms lazily over her head. "Lately every spare minute gets eaten by drills. The only downtime we get is on watch."

"Speak for yourself," I say. "My last night watch with Daren involved him making me balance on one heel, touch my nose with both index fingers, and spin like a top so he could inspect my aura alignment. Not making that up."

Leila laughs without a sound, her shoulders shaking.

"Could be worse," her blue script floats between us. "During my watch with Kate, she bolted upright in the dark and impaled three bats with ice spears because she convinced herself they were familiars sent by a hostile elementalist."

Her hand rests on her knee as the blue light fades.

"We should take a watch together sometime," she writes. "A quiet one. I promise not to make you balance on one foot... mostly."

choice("Agree to take the next night shift with Leila.", B3-Ch06c-Shift, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06c-Shift, v_b3_ch6_shift = 1)
choice("Tell her that I was planning to take the next night shift with Hadrik, to cheer him up.", B3-Ch06c-Shift, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06c-Shift, v_b3_ch6_shift = 0)


ID: B3-Ch06c-Shift
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch6_shift == 1) {
 "Sounds good to me," I say. "Taking the watch together, I mean. Not the acrobatic balancing."

"I guessed as much, but thanks for clarifying," Leila writes with a grin. "Second shift tonight, then?"

"Deal," I say.

"I'm looking forward to it," her script reads.

A quiet pause settles between us. Then new blue letters shape themselves in the air.

"My father is taking a long time to return," she writes, her brow furrowing.

"Do you have his transceiver frequency? I can call him."

Leila shakes her head. "I can't speak into a transceiver, so I never asked for his number."

"We can head back to the waiting room and ask around," I say. "There's only one corridor connecting this wing to the main hall—if he's on his way back, we'll run straight into him."

"Let's do that," she writes.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_shift == 0) {
 "I was actually planning to take the next watch with Hadrik," I say. "Thought he could use some company after all that."

"Don't worry," Leila writes quickly. "I said sometime, not tonight. Being there for a friend comes first."

"Thanks," I say. "Hopefully I can get him to crack a smile."

"I'm sure you will," she writes.

A quiet pause settles between us. Then new blue letters shape themselves in the air.

"My father is taking a long time to return," she writes, her brow furrowing.

"Do you have his transceiver frequency? I can call him."

Leila shakes her head. "I can't speak into a transceiver, so I never asked for his number."

"We can head back to the waiting room and ask around," I say. "There's only one corridor connecting this wing to the main hall—if he's on his way back, we'll run straight into him."

"Let's do that," she writes.

}
 We push ourselves up from the flagstones and retrace our steps down the passage. As we walk, Leila keeps her gaze fixed ahead, her fingers twisting the fabric of her sleeve.

"Do you think he's all right?" her glowing letters ask.

"He's fine," I tell her. "Knowing him, some piece of rebel machinery jammed and it was too heavy to haul back to his workbench."

"Right," she writes, the blue light fading quickly. "That's probably it."

We emerge into the waiting room and check with the rebels gathered near the benches, but none of them have crossed paths with Reidzek recently. Albert strolls over from the throne-room corridor.

"Doctor Reidzek?" Albert asks when we put the question to him. "He was tutoring our younger white mages on his enchanted healing gear a few hours ago. Then Barry called him over to run maintenance on the teleporter array. That was over an hour back. Hasn't he returned to his shop?"

"No," Leila writes. "We've been sitting outside his door the entire time."

"Array calibration can drag on," Albert says. "Want me to reach him on the transceiver?"

"Please," Leila writes. "If it's no trouble."

Albert unhooks his brass transceiver, dials the frequency, and taps the rune. "Doctor Reidzek, do you copy?" He pauses, frowning down at the small bronze plate. "Nothing. Channel's shut. He turns it off during delicate enchantments so nobody breaks his focus, but on standard maintenance? Give me a second. I'll dial Barry."

Albert shifts the dial and taps it again. He waits, but no tone returns.

"Busy signal," Albert mutters, annoyance creeping into his tone. "He's on an active line with someone else. We'll have to wait until he clears the—actually, hold on. Hey, Peter! Over here."

"On my way," Peter says, stepping into the waiting room from the main corridor. "What's going on?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06c-Herbs, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06c-Herbs)


ID: B3-Ch06c-Herbs
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch6_quarantine,1) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 1 && v_b3_ch6_secret >= 1 || v_b3_ch6_secret == 0 && v_b3_ch6_sleep == 0 && v_ch11_saved_rose == 1 || v_b3_ch6_password == 3 || v_b3_ch6_labels >= 2)
achievement("Quarantine ender",v_ac_b3_ch6_quarantine)
 "Doctor Reidzek hasn't returned to his workshop, and the last anyone saw of him, he was headed to the teleporter array for routine maintenance," Albert says. "Neither he nor Barry is answering their transceivers. Can you head down to the array room and check on them? Keep your channel open so you can alert us if something's wrong."

"Understood," Peter says. "I'll be right back."

"Thanks," Albert says. "And Peter—keep your aura concealed on the approach. Just in case."

"Got it," Peter says, his voice tightening. He turns and strides into the teleporter tunnel.

Albert glances down at Leila's tense expression. "Doctor Reidzek is fine. He gets absorbed in his work and forgets the hour, like always. Once Peter reaches the array, we'll have your father on the line."

"Yes," Leila writes, though her fingers tremble as she dismisses the script. "We just have to wait."

Across the hall, the heavy door to the quarantine ward swings open. Daren emerges, stepping over to Desmond, who remains stationed at his post.

#if(v_b3_ch6_labels < 2 && v_b3_ch6_password != 3 && v_ch11_saved_rose != 1 || v_b3_ch6_labels < 2 && v_b3_ch6_secret < 1 && v_b3_ch6_password != 3 && v_b3_ch6_secret != 0 || v_b3_ch6_labels < 2 && v_b3_ch6_secret < 1 && v_b3_ch6_password != 3 && v_b3_ch6_sleep != 0) {
 "Desmond, I'm... I'm sorry," Daren says, his head bowed. "There was no saving your brother. The sickness had taken too deep a hold. If I had arrived a few days earlier, he might have had a chance, but as things stand... he's too far gone. I'm sorry."

"It's all right," Desmond says with heavy resignation. "Don't tear yourself apart. You did everything you could. What about the others? Is there hope for any of them?"

"Half the ward is cured, including myself," Daren says. "For the rest, it depends entirely on how far the corruption spread before we administered the herbs. The critical cases won't pull through, but the moderate ones have a fighting chance. We'll know more once the medicine settles."

"Even half is a miracle," Desmond says. "Until today, anyone who crossed that threshold was as good as dead. Now we might not even need to quarantine future cases. You gave people their families back, healer. I'm the one who should apologize for doubting you. We're in your debt."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1 && v_b3_ch6_secret >= 1 || v_b3_ch6_secret == 0 && v_b3_ch6_sleep == 0 && v_ch11_saved_rose == 1 || v_b3_ch6_password == 3 || v_b3_ch6_labels >= 2) {
 "Desmond, your brother is out of danger," Daren says. "He'll need time to regain his strength—the infection ran deep—but he's going to live. You should be able to see him in a week or two."

"Are you serious?" Desmond stares at Daren, stunned disbelief written across his face. "No... that can't be. My brother was beyond saving! Are you certain it was him?"

"Two separate nurses confirmed he's the brother of the guard on duty," Daren says. "Unless you have a twin guarding this exact door?"

"No... I just..." Desmond's voice cracks, fighting to keep his composure. "Thank you. I never thought... I was sure you were another hopeless dreamer chasing windmills. What about the rest of the ward? How many did you pull through?"

"The vast majority are stabilized," Daren says. "A few severe cases are still fighting, but the nurses are managing them around the clock. Now I need to meet with your white mages to teach them the spells and herb preparations so they can handle any future outbreaks on their own."

"Then I won't hold you," Desmond says. "May the gods walk with you, healer. We're all in your debt."

Daren nods and turns toward the side corridor, catching sight of Leila and me as he crosses the floor.

"Sorry, no time to talk!" Daren calls out. "I need to find—"

"We heard you!" I shout back. "Go teach the healers. We'll be here!"

"Right!" he calls, disappearing down the passage.

Moments later, Peter's voice bursts through Albert's transceiver—ragged, loud, and choked with heavy breathing.

"Albert!" Peter gasps. "Albert, do you copy?!"

"Yes!" Albert responds sharply. "What happened? Where is Reidzek?"

}
 "I'm truly sorry about your brother," Daren says, his shoulders heavy with fatigue. "I wish I could have pulled everyone through. At least two of the nurses are back on their feet to keep the critical patients comfortable."

"You did more than anyone asked," Desmond says. "Go rest. You've earned it."

"Soon," Daren says. "Thank you."

He turns toward the side corridor, catching sight of Leila and me as he crosses the floor.

"Sorry, no time to talk!" Daren calls out. "I need to track down the rebels' white mages so I can teach them how to treat this sickness on their own!"

"Don't worry about us!" I shout back. "Go do what you need to. We'll be here!"

"Right!" he calls, disappearing down the passage.

Moments later, Peter's voice bursts through Albert's transceiver—ragged, loud, and choked with heavy breathing.

"Albert!" Peter gasps. "Albert, do you copy?!"

"Yes!" Albert responds sharply. "What happened? Where is Reidzek?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06c-Panic, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06c-Panic)


ID: B3-Ch06c-Panic
TEXT:

 "Albert!" Peter yells over the channel. "Barry betrayed us! I caught him on the transceiver with Gontrok in the array room! The King of Ollendor sanctioned a purge to wipe out everyone underground, and Barry's bringing them in! Warn the king! Fast!"

"Barry? Working with the guard?" Albert's voice hitches. "No—that's impossible. The guards murdered his brother—"

"His brother's alive!" Peter roars. "He lied from the start! The king's holding his brother hostage, that's why he broke! I heard Gontrok threatening to slaughter his brother if he didn't follow orders! And they took Doctor Reidzek! They forced Barry to teleport him to a seredium tower to seize control of the Overseer—because he's a lessathi mechanic who can handle ancient tech, just like Billy Bob!"

"What?!" Leila's glowing script flares in frantic, jagged strokes.

"They took Reidzek?" Albert demands. "We have to pull him out! Which tower did they—"

"Albert, shut up and listen!" Peter cuts him off. "Barry has been teleporting guard detachments into the cavern stratum directly above the base for an hour! They're rigging explosives to drop the ceiling and bury everyone alive! Sound the evacuation! Get everyone out before—ARGH!"

"Peter! What's happening?!" Albert yells into the device. "Peter?!"

"Guard trainees..." Peter grunts through heavy static. "Caught a bolt in the shoulder. I have to drop the channel. Move!"

The connection snaps shut.

"Damn it!" Albert roars. He rounds on a stunned rebel standing frozen nearby. "What are you gaping at? Sound the general alarm! Tell the king to clear the base immediately! I'm getting Peter!"

Albert blurs into thin air, sprinting down the passage toward the array room. The rebel sentry bolts down the main hall toward the throne room.

Leila breaks into a run, darting toward the teleporter corridor. I lunge forward and catch her forearm, dragging her to a halt.

"Leila, stop!"

"My father!" her blue letters flare, trembling violently. "I have to get to him!"

Terror widens her eyes, wiping all reason from her face—the exact soundless panic from Thilias when she was flailing in the dark water of the cistern, unable to scream.

"Leila, look at me," I say, planting my hands firmly on her shoulders. "We're going to get him back. Together. But charging down a collapsing tunnel gets us killed. Breathe. I need you thinking. Understand?"

She draws in a shuddering breath, her shoulders loosening beneath my grip. The blue script steadies. "Yes. Yes, I'm with you."

choice("Try to see if I can hear anything from the tunnel leading to the teleporter room.", B3-Ch06c-Hearing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06c-Hearing, v_b3_ch6_transceivers = 0)
choice("Try calling my friends through the transceivers to tell them what happened.", B3-Ch06c-Transceivers, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06c-Transceivers, v_b3_ch6_transceivers = 1)


ID: B3-Ch06c-Hearing
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch6_echoes,1) if (v_hearing >= 4)
set(v_b3_ch6_transceivers,0) if (v_hearing < 4)
set(v_b3_ch6_transceivers,2) if (v_hearing >= 4)
achievement("Do you hear an echo?",v_ac_b3_ch6_echoes)
#if(v_hearing < 4) {
 While Leila catches her breath, I strain to catch any sound carrying down the passage from the teleporter room. Where are the guards right now? Will they charge down the corridor, or drop through the ceiling from overhead? Were the trainees just trying to silence Peter, or is a full sweep coming this way?

Muffled shouts and clashing blades echo from the stone tunnel. I can't tell numbers, but the fighting is drawing closer with every heartbeat. The waiting room will turn into a killing floor in minutes. Can the rebels clear out their non-combatants in time?

No point getting bogged down in their defense. My job is getting Leila's father back. The rebels can hold their own tunnels.

"Barry?" Leila's script appears between us, steady now. "I'm ready. Let's call the others."

"Right," I say. "Daren should still be in the adjacent ward. I'll get him on the line."

I pull out my transceiver and work the brass dial. My fingers tremble enough that I overshoot the notch twice before clicking into Daren's frequency. So much for preaching composure.

Before my thumb can tap the contact rune, a deafening crack rips through the stone overhead. The ceiling fractures, dumping jagged rocks and blinding dust into the room.

}
#if(v_hearing >= 4) {
 While Leila catches her breath, I focus my hearing on the dark passage leading to the array chamber.

Beyond the distant scrape of boots and muffled shouts, a faint, metallic rasp vibrates through the rock—Gontrok's voice carrying over a transceiver speaker.

"Save your excuses!" Gontrok barks. "Teleport her now!"

"She's too agitated!" Barry Thundercloud's frantic voice replies. "Her aura is fluctuating wild! If I force a lock while her signature is unstable, she could scatter across three different caverns—"

"She's our leverage!" Gontrok snarls. "If we want that lessathi mechanic working the tower controls, his daughter has to be locked in the castle dungeon! You have the cell coordinates. Lock on and pull her through!"

"I don't have a direct line to her transceiver!" the operator stammers. "Triangulating her manual signature takes—give me a minute!"

I turn to Leila. "This is going to sound insane, but I need you to panic again."

Her blue script freezes in midair, utterly bewildered. "What?"

"They're trying to lock a teleport beam onto you from the array room," I say. "An unstable aura makes the targeting slip. Keep your signature chaotic. Understand?"

"I... yes," she writes, her eyes widening. "I think I can—"

A deafening blast tears through the stone directly overhead. The ceiling fractures, dumping jagged rocks and blinding dust into the room.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch06c-Ending, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06c-Ending)


ID: B3-Ch06c-Transceivers
TEXT:

 While Leila catches her breath, I pull out my transceiver to rally the group. My fingers shake badly enough that I overshoot the frequency notch twice before locking onto Daren's channel. So much for preaching composure.

I tap the contact rune. "Daren, do you copy?"

"Here with the white mages," Daren answers immediately. "What's happened?"

"No time for details—listen carefully," I say. "The teleporter operator sold out the base. He's been dropping royal guard units into the cavern stratum overhead for an hour to bring the ceiling down on us. On top of that, they grabbed Doctor Reidzek and hauled him to a seredium tower to force him onto the Overseer's controls. Get to the waiting room now. I'll alert the others."

"Understood," Daren says, skipping questions. "Reach Kate and Flower. I'll flag down Hadrik."

"Right. Move."

I switch channels, buzz Kate, and rattle off the warning as fast as words will come. Before I can dial Flower, an ear-splitting detonation tears through the rock overhead. The chamber shudders, and heavy stone blocks tear free from the ceiling.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06c-Ending, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06c-Ending)


ID: B3-Ch06c-Ending
TEXT:

 "Look out!" I grab Leila's wrist and yank her backward as a massive slab of stone crashes into the floor where she stood.

Dust billows into our faces. "That was too close—"

Before the words clear my mouth, Leila grabs my tunic and hauls me sideways. A second cascade of rock smashes into the flagstones at my heels. Secondary detonations roar throughout the stratum overhead, reverberating through the tunnels as support pillars crumble.

While Leila is still clutching my arm, a blinding white glow wraps around us. The resonance rattles through our bones and teeth—the unmistakable pull of a forced teleportation field wrenching us away from the collapsing base.

#if(v_b3_ch6_transceivers == 2) {
 The white glare snaps shut. Damp, dead air rushes into my lungs. The jagged crystalline outcroppings and moss-slick stone walls are immediately familiar—the exact dead-end cavern where the failsafe dump deposited us yesterday when fleeing the black-robed guard. Why here again? Is this cavern the default discharge point whenever teleport targeting fails?

I don't get time to work it out. The sickening backlash of a botched teleport hits my skull like a warhammer. My vision spins violently, cold nausea clawing up my throat. My knees buckle, the stone floor rushing up to meet me.

Through the static closing over my eyes, frantic, oversized blue letters burn in the gloom:

"Barry, can you read this? Please answer me! Barry!"

The blue glow smears into black, and my forehead strikes the cold stone.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_transceivers != 2) {
 The white glare snaps shut. Damp, dead air rushes into my lungs. The jagged crystalline outcroppings and moss-slick stone walls are immediately familiar—the exact dead-end cavern where the failsafe dump deposited us yesterday when fleeing the black-robed guard. Why here again? Is this cavern the default discharge point whenever teleport targeting fails? And why wrench just the two of us clear instead of letting the ceiling bury us with the rebels?

I don't get time to work it out. The sickening backlash of a botched teleport hits my skull like a warhammer. My vision spins violently, cold nausea clawing up my throat. My knees buckle, the stone floor rushing up to meet me.

Through the static closing over my eyes, frantic, oversized blue letters burn in the gloom:

"Barry, can you read this? Please answer me! Barry!"

The blue glow smears into black, and my forehead strikes the cold stone.

}
